      "id": "chapter-13",
      "number": 13,
      "title": "Chapter 13 — Integrate a low-pressure product mention",
      "paragraphs": [
        "13.1 The source's approach\nThe transcript recommends avoiding aggressive sales pitches. In its example, Ruth briefly mentions that viewers have asked for the recipes in one place, explains that she has collected her favourites into a guide, and points viewers to the link below before returning to the episode.",
        "The principle is to make the offer relevant, brief, and optional.\n13.2 Example of a transparent product mention\n“If you'd like the recipes from this episode collected in one place, I've put together a kitchen guide with the full instructions and a few extra ideas. You'll find the details in the description. Now, let's get back to today's recipe.”",
        "Only say viewers have requested something if they actually have. If the product is newly created, say so honestly.\n13.3 Placement\nThe source demonstrates a mention around the middle of the video. The exact placement should depend on the episode:\n- Give the viewer useful content first.\n- Introduce the product where it naturally supports the topic.\n- Keep the mention short.\n- Make the link easy to find in the description.\n- Include a clear written product description.\n- Avoid interrupting an important safety explanation.\n13.4 Adaptable prompt: natural product mention\nWrite a brief, low-pressure product mention for this episode.",
        "Episode topic: [topic].\nProduct: [product name and what it contains].\nRelevant viewer need: [need].\nLink placement: video description.",
        "Keep it conversational and transparent. Do not claim viewers requested it unless that is true. Do not exaggerate the product's benefits, create false urgency, or imply that buying it is necessary. Return three short options."
      ]
