      "paragraphs": [
        "14.1 The source's example calculation\nThe transcript uses this hypothetical funnel:\n- Video views: 100,000.\n- Link click rate: 1%.\n- Product-page visitors: 1,000.\n- Purchase conversion rate: 3%.\n- Purchases: 30.\n- Product price: $37.\nCalculation:\n100,000 * 1% = 1,000 visitors\n1,000 * 3% = 30 purchases\n30 * $37 = $1,110",
        "The $1,110 is gross product sales before payment fees, platform fees, refunds, taxes, advertising, production costs, and other expenses. It is not profit.\n14.2 Why the monthly headline cannot be inferred from one example\nThe video title references $12,804 per month, but the supplied transcript does not provide a verifiable monthly revenue statement, detailed sales ledger, channel analytics, expense breakdown, or evidence that the hypothetical funnel occurred.",
        "The transcript also presents a three-videos-per-week scenario and extrapolates a much larger annual figure if every video performs at the assumed level. That is a scenario, not a dependable forecast. Videos do not all receive 100,000 views, and click and purchase rates vary.\n14.3 Build your own scenario model\nVariable\tConservative\tBase case\tOptimistic\nMonthly views\t10,000\t50,000\t100,000\nLink click rate\t0.5%\t1%\t1.5%\nProduct page visits\t50\t500\t1,500\nPurchase conversion\t1%\t2%\t3%\nExpected orders\t0.5\t10\t45\nProduct price\t$19\t$29\t$37\nGross sales\t$9.50\t$290\t$1,665",
        "This table is a sensitivity illustration, not a forecast. Fractional orders represent expected values over repeated periods, not literal partial purchases. Real results may be lower or higher.\n14.4 Track revenue and profit separately\nRecord:\n- Gross product sales.\n- Refunds.\n- Payment and platform fees.\n- Taxes collected or owed.\n- Software subscriptions.\n- AI generation costs.\n- Editing and production costs.\n- Affiliate or contractor costs.\n- Net operating profit.\n- Hours spent producing and supporting the channel.\nA channel can generate substantial sales and still have weak profit if production costs, refunds, and time requirements are high."
      ]
